# FIXED DEFECT, 2026-08-04. Symptom: `panic: 3 heap blocks still live at exit (a
# missing decref)` on the first Heroes program that contained a string. Cause: the
# ownership pass classified a value as owning by looking at its defining operation,
# and `+` on a `str` is an `Op::Binary` — it reads like arithmetic and it is a
# constructor. §4.14's operator table puts `str + str` in the same row as `int + int`,
# so only the RESULT TYPE tells them apart. Fix: `own.rs::allocates` returns true for
# `Binary`, which it can only reach when the result is refcounted.
#
# All three leaked blocks traced to that one row: one per `greet` call (the returned
# concatenation) plus the `s + s` in main. Named after the defect, per Go's
# `test/fixedbugs` (CLAUDE.md §9).
#
# What makes this case a test rather than an anecdote: the leak counter in the shim.
# AddressSanitizer reports NOTHING here — `detect_leaks is not supported on this
# platform` — which is why panel 021 replaced it as the leak gate.

function greet(name: str) -> str
    return "hi " + name

function main()
    print(greet("Ziggy"))
    s = "ab"
    print(s + s)
    out: str @ ""
    i: int @ 0
    while i < 200
        out @ out + "x"
        i @ i + 1
    print(len(out))
